The within-pool slope ($-$0.401, CI excludes zero) is statistically indistinguishable from the independent 16-relation pool ($-$0.509; permutation $p \approx 0.60$). The smaller quartile gap ($-$3.92\,pp vs.\ $-$5.31\,pp) reflects the narrower BCS range within the 80-relation pool (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_within_pool}).

In the full 80-relation condition, natural BCS variation is insufficient (IQR${}=0.033$) to yield a detectable effect ($n=25$, slope${}=+0.221$, $p \approx 0.76$). This does not invalidate the confusability effect---the within-pool slope is significant ($p=0.027$) when BCS is deliberately varied---but establishes that natural schema sampling at this scale does not produce sufficient BCS contrast for detection.

\section{SCS Embedding Ablation: Name-Only vs. Name+Definition}
\label{app:scs-ablation}

Table~\ref{tab:scs-ablation} compares predictive accuracy of name-only SCS against name+definition SCS across three datasets.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Dataset & SCS$_\text{name}$ $r$ & SCS$_\text{def}$ $r$ & $\Delta r$ & Matrix corr. \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & 0.172 & 0.149 & $-$0.023 & 0.894 \\
MAVEN & 0.129 & 0.164 & +0.035 & 0.656 \\
FewRel & 0.178 & 0.193 & +0.015 & 0.848 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Pearson correlation between SCS and empirical confusion rate. ``Def'' concatenates type name with definition before embedding. All correlations significant ($p < 0.001$). The inconsistent direction of $\Delta r$ indicates no systematic benefit from including definitions.}
\label{tab:scs-ablation}
\end{table}

Few-NERD is the only dataset whose prompts include type definitions; for MAVEN and FewRel, prompts use type names alone, making name-only SCS a direct match to model input. The high matrix correlation (0.66--0.89) confirms that type names capture the dominant semantic structure.

\section{Pair-to-Batch Aggregation Simulation}
\label{app:aggregation-sim}

We verify that the pair-level $R^2 \approx 7\%$ (computed over non-zero confusion pairs) can aggregate to the observed batch-level $R^2 \approx 22\%$ via a Monte Carlo simulation.

\paragraph{Setup.} From the FewRel 16-relation experiment, we estimate the inter-pair SCS correlation for pairs sharing a common type ($\rho_\text{SCS} = 0.35$). For batches of $k=8$ types ($\binom{8}{2}=28$ pairs), we simulate 10{,}000 iterations: (1) generate 28 correlated pair-level effects with individual $R^2 = 0.074$; (2) average to obtain batch-level signals; (3) compute batch-level $R^2$.

\paragraph{Results.} Simulated batch $R^2 = 0.219$ [95\% CI: 0.092--0.361], matching the observed value of 0.218. The effective amplification factor is $\sim$3$\times$, explained by signal correlation within batches preserving variance while independent noise averages out (effective degrees of freedom $\approx 3.4$, not 28).

\section{Cross-Domain Connections}
\label{app:cross-domain}

The schema-induced degradation documented here is not unique to information extraction. Schema-Guided Dialogue systems (SGD/SGD-X; \citealt{rastogi2020towards}) face an analogous challenge: as the number of intent and slot types grows, dialogue state tracking accuracy declines. \citet{li2023evaluating} observed count-dependent degradation in ChatGPT-based IE, attributing it to prompt overload. Our two-channel decomposition offers a more refined explanation: schema size per se is not the sole driver; rather, larger schemas increase the probability of including high-SCS pairs, which contribute disproportionately to confusion. This distinction has practical implications---a schema of 80 types with low mutual confusability may outperform a 40-type schema with clustered semantics---and suggests that confusability-aware design principles may generalize to any task where an LLM must discriminate among a prompt-specified label inventory.

\end{document}

\section{Schema Decomposition: Failure, Diagnosis, and Mitigation}
\label{sec:sdgd}

A natural intervention against confusability-driven degradation is to partition the schema so that confusable types never co-occur in the same prompt. We test this idea with \textbf{Schema-DAG-Guided Decomposition} (SDGD\footnote{SDGD (Schema Decomposition and Grouping for Degradation mitigation) is an adopted abbreviation for schema decomposition and grouping; the graph structure is undirected.}), which uses greedy graph coloring on the pairwise SCS graph to assign types to low-confusability batches.

We construct a thresholded confusability graph $G = (V, E)$ where each type is a vertex and an edge connects types $t_i, t_j$ whenever $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j) > \tau$. Greedy graph coloring (\textsc{largest\_first}) assigns each type to a color such that no two adjacent---i.e., highly confusable---types share a batch. Each color class forms one extraction batch. For NER, predictions from all batches are merged via set union; for RE, each instance is routed to the batch containing its gold type (oracle routing). We test four NER modes: (i)~\textbf{single\_pass}, the undecomposed baseline; (ii)~\textbf{sdgd\_zero}, SCS-guided partitioning; (iii)~\textbf{random\_batch}, random partitioning with matched batch sizes; and (iv)~\textbf{per\_type}, each type extracted in isolation. Every mode falls below the single-pass NER baseline: SDGD obtains F1\,=\,0.251, random batching 0.320, per-type 0.292, versus 0.445 for single-pass (Table~\ref{tab:sdgd_results}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sdgd}).

\paragraph{Merge amplification in NER.}
The core failure mechanism is \emph{merge amplification}: presenting the same text to the model $k$ times with different type subsets causes each pass to independently generate entity predictions, and union merge accumulates all of them into a single output. Each pass produces an independent set of span predictions; because LLM outputs are not calibrated across passes \cite{llm-calibration-survey-2024}, the same span may receive conflicting type labels from different passes, or hallucinated entities may appear in passes where the correct type is absent. Union merge retains all predictions, so false positives scale with $k$ while true positives are bounded by the fixed set of gold entities.

Quantitatively, single-pass precision (0.563) collapses to 0.210--0.249 after union merge across 8 batches, while recall gains are modest (0.368 $\to$ 0.314 for sdgd\_zero, 0.446 for random batch). The net F1 loss is substantial: 0.445 $\to$ 0.251 (sdgd\_zero) or 0.320 (random batch). Per-type extraction (66 batches) pushes recall to 0.586 but precision to 0.194 (F1\,=\,0.292), confirming that the precision--recall imbalance intensifies with the number of passes. Random batching outperforms confusability-aware SDGD on NER (0.320 vs.\ 0.251) because the dominant degradation source---merge amplification---is independent of batch composition; any multi-pass scheme suffers from it regardless of grouping quality.

\paragraph{Confidence gating does not resolve merge amplification.}
Confidence-based gating (retaining only predictions above threshold) trades recall for precision but does not recover net F1 (0.251$\to$0.241), confirming that merge artifacts are systemic rather than confined to low-confidence predictions (Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_sdgd}).

\paragraph{Why NER fails but RE does not.}
Merge amplification is specific to span-level tasks. Each NER pass independently proposes entity spans, and union merge must resolve conflicting boundaries and type assignments, degrading systematically with pass count. RE avoids this mechanism entirely: under oracle routing, each relation instance receives a single classification from the batch containing its gold type, requiring no span reconciliation and eliminating the multi-pass interference that causes SDGD to fail on NER. This makes RE a clean testbed for evaluating whether confusability-aware decomposition improves accuracy once merge artifacts are removed.

\paragraph{Oracle routing as performance ceiling.}
Oracle routing routes each test instance to the batch containing its gold type, eliminating cross-type confusion within each batch. On FewRel 80-relation RE, oracle-routed decomposition achieves 0.802 accuracy versus 0.504 for the single-pass baseline---a gain of +29.8\,pp (Table~\ref{tab:batch_composition}). This is the strongest positive finding of the decomposition experiments: it demonstrates that type confusability is indeed a key driver of degradation in RE, and that performance recovers substantially when confusable types are separated.

However, oracle routing requires gold labels and is not deployable. The gap between the oracle ceiling and practical routing reveals an important asymmetry: while \emph{any} decomposition benefits enormously from removing the 80-type bottleneck (+28.4\,pp for random batching, +29.8\,pp for graph coloring), the \emph{additional} benefit of confusability-aware grouping over random grouping is modest (+1.4\,pp, $p \approx 0.19$). The confusability-aware strategy ranks 13th out of 15 random seeds, limited by the narrow BCS margin (0.006) between graph-coloring and random batches at this partition granularity. Finer-grained partitioning or alternative confusability metrics may be needed to translate the oracle-demonstrated potential into significant practical gains.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Condition} & \textbf{Accuracy} & \textbf{Mean BCS} \\
\midrule
Single-pass (80 types) & 0.504 & 0.557 \\
Random batching (15 seeds)$^\dagger$ & 0.788\,$\pm$\,0.018 & 0.559 \\
Confus.-aware (graph coloring) & \textbf{0.802} & 0.552 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Batch composition on FewRel 80-relation RE with oracle routing. $^\dagger$Mean $\pm$ SD across 15 random seeds. Graph coloring yields +1.4\,pp over random batching ($p \approx 0.19$, n.s.) with a narrow BCS margin (0.006). The +29.8\,pp gap between single-pass and decomposed conditions confirms that type separation recovers performance when merge amplification is absent.}
\label{tab:batch_composition}
\end{table}

